// STATUS 2026-08-15: no longer an engine divergence — after the
// kernel frame-init fix BOTH engines Timeout (the jit's in-frame
// const fire was what let it produce values; both now read the
// program as non-terminating and grind). The oracle scores
// both-Timeout as AGREE. What remains is purely the semantics
// questions below, still awaiting Eric — question 2's depth-trip
// settle now applies to BOTH engines.
// Originally: asymmetric timeout (interp exceeds budget; jit
// produces) — parked 2026-08-14, found by soak aug14d (katana fuzz
// divergence_000000) BEFORE the arm-local seam fixes.
//
// The recursive call's ARGUMENT is `{let s = i64:0; s <- fold(…); s}`.
// Now that a `<-` target's seed applies once (arm-local-bind-aug2026),
// s advances 0 -> 6, so f(6) recurses on 6 forever: the program IS
// non-terminating under the current reading.
//
// TRIAGED TO GROUND 2026-08-15 — the old question 2's premise was
// wrong: the recursion is TAIL, so the 256 call-depth limit CAN
// NEVER fire (tail loops accrue no depth; that is the point of the
// optimization). What actually happens (GXDBG_TAIL): after emitting
// 1, 1, 2 the interp's tail driver spins in-frame at ~17k passes/sec,
// every pass re-deriving f(6) and pending another f(6) jump — the
// runtime is WEDGED mid-cycle; the jit native-loops the same way.
// Both engines have the containment hook already (the interp driver
// polls ctx.interrupted() per pass, node/lambda.rs ~641; kernels
// poll at loop heads), but the ONLY arming site is
// GXHandle::interrupt() — nothing arms it automatically, so an
// un-instrumented embedder (the shell, the fuzz child) hangs until
// killed from outside.
//
// The questions for Eric, sharpened:
//   1. is seed-applies-once inside a call argument the intended
//      reading (the program is then legally non-terminating)?
//      Precedent says yes — re-seeding per pass would break the
//      accumulator idiom the tail frames exist for — so this may
//      just be confirmation.
//   2. what is the CONTAINMENT story for an infinite tail loop:
//      (a) status quo — embedder-armed interrupt only, a hostile or
//          buggy program wedges anyone who doesn't arm it;
//      (b) the tail driver yields per runtime cycle (one pass per
//          cycle, like a `x <- x + 1` counter — the runtime stays
//          live and responsive, the program legally spins);
//      (c) an iteration budget per derivation that settles to the
//          whole-derivation bottom, like the depth trip does for
//          non-tail recursion.
// The kernel meanwhile produces values, and which values it produces
// changed with the seed rule ([1,2,3,4] before, [1,1,2,2] after).
{let x = array::iter([i64:1, i64:2, i64:3, i64:4]); let m = x / i64:3; let rec f = |n: i64| -> i64 select n {i64:0 => select i64:0 {i64:0 if m == i64:0 => i64:1, _ => i64:2}, _ => f({let s = i64:0; s <- array::fold([i64:1, i64:2, i64:3], i64:0, |acc, e| acc + e); s})}; f(m)}
